WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.420
The first stationary bike was developed by a
London physician in the late 18th century to

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:10.400
exercise the joints of patients suffering
from gout, rheumatic disorders or other

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:16.310
afflictions. The modern stationary bike is
all about helping people stay fit to prevent

00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:26.160
illnesses. On this stationary bike, magnets
linked to the flywheel create tension that

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.680
causes the cyclist to push harder, making
the workout truly an uphill battle.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.580
Production starts with steel tubing for the
base.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.080
A probe locates the center of the tubing.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:45.990
This reference point allows a computerized
laser cutter to find the exact spots to cut

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.830
assembly holes with the assembly holes cut.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.530
The laser slices the tubing to the correct
length.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:58.260
An automated system retrieves the part and
transfers it to a holding area.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:05.160
A worker inserts screws in the base part,
which will attach it to the main frame.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.640
He pipes sealant around the screws.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:16.860
Another worker now arranges the three
tubular base parts in a welding fixture and

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.390
clamps them in place.

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:23.880
The fixture revolves and meets up with a
robotic welder.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.570
The robot welds the three parts together.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:30.930
This completes the base of the stationary
bike.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:37.110
The bike's mainframe has been configured on
the other side of the welding fixture.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:40.110
The robot welds these parts together.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:45.150
The fixture rotates to serve up another base
assembly to the robot, and the worker

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:48.180
collects the welded mainframe from the
reverse side.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:54.900
He grinds the welded seams smooth and gets
rid of burrs on the steel.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.450
Workers sandblast the metal, wash it, and
apply primer.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:06.990
After grounding the mainframe, another
worker sprays electrostatically charged resin

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:08.490
and pigment onto it.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:13.140
Once it's baked on, this powder coat will
provide a protective finish.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.980
At another station, a 55 ton press punches
holes into posts for the bike's handlebar and

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.270
seat. The holes are for adjusting the height
of these parts.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.170
Next up is the casting for mounting the
handlebar to the post.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:36.780
Using another press, the worker entrenches
the top of the post in the casting.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:43.440
He taps the casting with a rubber mallet to
tweak the installation and sets the assembly

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:51.000
aside. At another station, a worker arranges
numerous seat posts in tight alignment.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.160
He drapes a stencil of sequential lettering
on top of the posts.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:02.280
He dips an etching wand in a chemical
solution and then moves it over the letters

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:03.480
five times.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.240
This burns the letters into the steel,
providing markers for height adjustment.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:16.590
An assembler drives a wedge into one of the
bike's two crank arms to create a hole that

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.180
the axle will fit into.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.120
He checks the depth of the hole with a
gauge.

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:28.620
He inserts the end of the axle in the hole
and, using a press, pushes it into place.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:35.490
He measures the hole from the other side of
the crank arm, and confirms that the axle has

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.200
been set to the correct depth.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.930
He then bolts the assembly to a precise
torque.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:48.390
He locks the crank arm assembly in a lathe.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:54.540
He slips a foam sleeve over the axle to
protect it from shards of flying metal.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:59.430
As a tool machines the part of the crank arm
that will ultimately hold the bike's pulley.

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:08.490
Another member of the team now assembles
bearings to a wheel hub.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.790
He slides two bearings with a spacer in a
press.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:19.430
He heats the hub in an oven, causing the
metal to expand.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.460
The machine presses the hub sandwich
together.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:27.770
As the hub cools, it shrinks for a tight fit
to the bearings.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:32.510
He installs a retaining clip in the hub to
also keep it together.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.300
Next up are the holders for the magnets that
create resistance on the flywheel.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:42.980
A worker places each one in a fixture to
inspect the dimensions.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:50.000
He then checks the strength of the magnets
in a tester and satisfied.

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:52.130
He inserts two in each holder.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:05:00.140
This metal cage pulled into position by the
magnets completes the magnetic field.

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:04.610
Stay tuned for more on the making of this
stationary bike.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:10.720
The assembler installs the magnetic
resistance unit so that it swings from a

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.310
bracket on the main frame.

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:15.640
This bracket also holds the computer board.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.820
He mounts the aluminum flywheel to the axle,
sliding it between the magnets and the

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:22.960
tension device.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:27.670
He caps the flywheel hub and secures the cap
with screws.

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:34.690
The torsion spring equipped cover completes
the stationary bikes tension system.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:42.680
He assembles the crank, arm and axle to the
bike and installs a bearing.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:45.620
Then the bike moves on to the next station.

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:50.180
Another worker places an alignment tool on
the back axle.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:56.630
He loops an elastic belt with grooves around
the small pulley on the flywheel, and around

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:59.180
the larger crank arm pulley linking the two.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:04.910
He inserts another tool into the crank arm
and turns it to better wrap the belt around

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:06.410
the large crank pulley.

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:11.000
He then removes both installation tools.

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:17.720
He installs four flat bushings in the
handlebar post.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:22.190
These bushings make it possible for the
handlebar to be raised and lowered.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:28.850
He applies a decal with the height
indicators made of a rugged synthetic

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:34.670
material. The decal encases three sides of
the metal post and this protects it from

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:44.370
abrasion. He bolts the second crank arm to
the axle and torques it to the

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:45.840
specified tension.

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:52.200
Next up is the seat assembly.

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:57.840
He slides the seat stem into the post and
tightens the adjustment knob to lock it in

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:06.090
place. He inserts the handlebar post into
the bike frame and fishes the gear, cable and

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:08.130
communication wires through it.

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:12.960
He secures the handlebar post with the
adjustment knob.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:19.530
He connects the communication line to the
resistance system's computer board.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:25.740
He links the gear cable to the resistance
system and tests its functionality.

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:31.290
Satisfied, he tightens a nut at the side to
secure the cable.

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:35.640
Another worker installs a plastic cover on
the drive train.

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:40.230
This will protect the cyclist's legs from
the pulley system and shield the metal

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.030
components from human sweat.

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:48.360
For added moisture protection, she applies
sealer around a welded reinforcement on the

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:54.540
bike frame. She then encases the fork of the
bike with more molded plastic.

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:59.610
Bolts. Secure the casings to the bike.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:08.580
She now places a magnetized microphone on
the axle and spins the flywheel at a high

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:11.520
rpm. This is a test.

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:19.140
The bike must operate noiselessly and the
decibel meter confirms the noise level is

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:24.540
negligible. She also feels the frame for
unwanted vibrations.

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:30.510
She now sets the location of the magnetic
resistance mechanism using a special tool.

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:36.630
This calibrates the resistance so that the
handlebar computer can find it and display

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:38.820
the resistance setting during cycling.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:45.090
Another member of the team then applies
thread locking adhesive to the screw holes in

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:46.290
the crank arms.

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:52.980
He screws the pedals tightly to the crank
arms and torques them to a specific setting.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:02.140
The worker aligns the four bolt holes on the
frame to the bolts on the base, and secures

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:03.550
them with cap nuts.

